# Forgejo Actions runner registration token — plaintext TEMPLATE. The real
# file is nixos/forgejo-runner.sops.env, encrypted to the production age
# recipient. See nixos/renovate.sops.env.example for why these two secrets
# are dotenv files rather than keys in nixos/secrets.sops.yaml.
#
# The key MUST be spelled `TOKEN`: nixpkgs' gitea-actions-runner module wires
# `tokenFile` straight into `EnvironmentFile=`, and its ExecStartPre reads
# `$TOKEN`. The decrypted file is therefore already exactly the artifact
# systemd wants — no sops.templates bridge needed.
#
# TO FILL IN (one operator step, once Forgejo is up):
#
#   1. Forgejo -> Site Administration -> Actions -> Runners -> Create new
#      runner. Copy the registration token.
#      (A repository-level token under repo Settings -> Actions -> Runners
#      scopes the runner to one repo instead; either works.)
#   2. sopsedit nixos/forgejo-runner.sops.env
#   3. nixos-rebuild switch --target-host idan@10.0.0.3 --use-remote-sudo \
#        --flake .#services-vm
#
# The token is ONE-SHOT: act_runner exchanges it for a durable credential in
# /var/lib/gitea-runner/forgejo/.runner and never uses it again. Rotating the
# value here forces nothing by itself — the module's ExecStartPre only
# re-registers when .runner is missing or the label list changed.
#
# Until step 2 happens, ci-secrets.nix writes no runtime file and
# gitea-runner-forgejo.service is SKIPPED by ConditionPathExists rather than
# entering a registration retry loop.

# Registration token from Forgejo's runner-creation dialog.
TOKEN=changeme_forgejo_runner_registration_token
